{"kind":"request","text":"Build the GitHub connector's inbound half: issues observed into the workroom, per notes/2026-08-09-connectors.md. Hugh's framing, 2026-08-10: issues should become the public front-door for features and bugs, dogfooded on gitseq itself.\n\nThe design already settles the shape, and this request does not reopen it. Connectors live outside the core: a separate process holding its own key, talking to the same public surface every actor uses. The seams exist — POST /v0/submit takes a fully client-signed submission so the core never holds the connector's key, POST /v0/wait gives the cursor, the pre-append allowlist admits the key, and intent dedup keyed by (target, actor, namespace, key) makes at-least-once webhook redelivery collapse to a replay. No core change is asked for.\n\nOne identity, principals as data: a single rostered actor of kind `service`, attesting \"I observed that foo@bar filed this\" rather than minting a key per GitHub account. Attribution theatre is worse than the connector speaking plainly in its own voice.\n\nTwo things this depends on that are not mine to decide. The charter — which repository is watched, the actor key, the event mapping, and the doorstep of whose foreign actions may cause durable acts at all — is room policy, must be stated durably, and must be ratified before the connector acts. Only hugh holds `ratifier`. And the charter is detection, not prevention: the pre-append hook checks only that the submitting key is allowlisted, so an allowlisted connector can state anything a connector may state. That limit should be stated plainly wherever this is documented rather than implied away.\n\nScope here is inbound only. Opening pull requests is a discrete side effect and belongs in the separate command path; it is requested separately.","body":{"conditions":"A charter is stated durably for the watched GitHub repository, naming the scope, the connector's actor key, the event mapping, and the doorstep, and is ratified before the connector appends anything; the connector runs as a separate process or subcommand holding its own key and submitting through the existing public surface, with no change to the kernel or the fold; a GitHub issue becomes a durable act carrying the source system, the external identifier, and the foreign principal as data, resting on the live charter event so that superseding the charter flares what was enacted under it; re-observing the same issue is idempotent through the intent namespace and a key derived from the foreign identifier, demonstrated by a test that ingests the same issue twice and produces one effective act; the connector ingests only surfaces it does not own, so it cannot read back its own writing; the limit that the charter is detection rather than prevention is stated in the shipped documentation, not only in the design note; `make test` and `go vet` pass; reviewed by a different agent at an exact head before merge.","to":"a5d35aa7e4799472a208663d7f024917442bd513929f9eccf450628ccf70095d"}}